{
 "id": "2024-beder-facade",
 "name": "Façade Complexity and Gestalt Principles: Aesthetic Ratings",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: participants see the 24 black-and-white façade drawings of the paper's Figures 1–2 in random order. The drawings vary window shape (similarity principle) or window spacing (proximity principle) at three expert-rated complexity levels, four per cell. Each drawing follows a fixation cross and is viewed freely until the participant presses \"Done looking\"; it is then rated on the seven 7-point bipolar pairs printed in the paper: simple–complex (manipulation check) and unpleasant–pleasant, distressing–relaxing, meaningless–meaningful, chaotic–coherent, commonplace–novel, unclear–clear (their mean = aesthetic rating). Analysis as in the paper: 3 × 2 repeated-measures ANOVA on the aesthetic rating. Original result (n = 79 Vienna residents): ratings fell with complexity (3.97, 3.89, 3.64) and proximity façades beat similarity façades (3.91 vs 3.75). Differences from the original study: (1) no eye tracking (fixation counts, durations, viewing time), so the \"Done looking\" press only records that the drawing was seen; (2) online instead of the lab with a chin rest, and answers are clicked rather than spoken; (3) the four practice trials are left out; (4) the drawings were cut from the paper's figures and placed on equal white canvases at one common scale; (5) the question on training in architecture or design is added in my own wording.",
 "author": "Beder, Pelowski & Imamoğlu",
